window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-188ef0f419dbb622"]={"key":"ny_b1-188ef0f419dbb622","group":"ny_b1","uid":"188ef0f419dbb622","rank":26,"title":"kestrel felix tribute treatment edit map","occupation":"audio_video_technicians","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Ayana Zieliński, the editor in Kestrel Collective’s in-house media unit. Felix Bergqvist is stepping down in October after a cardiac diagnosis, and Ivo Salcedo, chair of the Gallowspring Trust board, wants a twelve-minute tribute film ready for Felix’s send-off and suitable to remain on the Kestrel Collective site afterwards. The only source material is the complete interview package in `kestrel_felix_interviews.docx`, which contains nine hours of conversations with people who worked with Felix; there is no existing template or previous cut to follow. Prepare `kestrel_felix_tribute_treatment_and_edit_map.docx` and put it at the top level of the working directory, using exactly that filename. The document is for Ivo Salcedo and the board to approve before anything is assembled, but it must also be practical enough for the edit team to use without asking you what the intended film is. Keep the proposed finished runtime at exactly twelve minutes, excluding any optional end-card hold that you clearly identify as optional. 1) Set out a clear creative concept and give the film one authored organising idea rather than presenting a collection of interview clips; explain why that shape suits Felix, the send-off room, and later web viewing. 2) Provide a concise audience and tone statement, including how the film should acknowledge Felix’s health situation without turning the tribute into a medical story or making claims the interviews do not support. 3) Identify the principal voices and the editorial role each one plays, and distinguish essential voices from useful reserves. 4) Map the complete twelve minutes in time-coded sections, with start and end times that reconcile to the stated runtime, showing the intended voice, subject, visual treatment, transitions, music or natural sound, and purpose of every section. 5) Make the map specific enough to guide a first assembly: indicate the likely interview selections by speaker and topic or distinctive wording from the source, while avoiding invented quotations and marking any wording that still requires a source check. 6) Show how the film opens, develops, reaches an emotional or argumentative turn, and closes; do not let the structure depend on chronology unless you can make that choice persuasive. 7) Include a restrained visual and audio language for archival material, photographs, room sound, music, captions, and name identifiers, with accessibility and intelligibility considered for a public web version. 8) Deal explicitly with the fact that Felix will be in the room: identify choices that preserve his dignity, avoid an ambush, and keep the emotional register affectionate rather than clinically or sentimentally exploitative. 9) Cormac Macaulay has already promised Felix that every person interviewed will appear in the finished film. Address that promise in a professional way, stating what can and cannot responsibly be guaranteed before the edit is assembled and how contributors can still be acknowledged without weakening the film. 10) The unit has nine hours of interviews but only a short preparation window before the board meeting, so a full transcript-and-paper-edit workflow is not possible. Set out a defensible lightweight logging or selection fallback, including what must still be checked before picture lock and what uncertainty the board should understand. 11) Give a short approval note identifying the main editorial tradeoff, the decision the board is being asked to approve, and the material risks of changing the structure late. Keep the document to no more than six pages, use plain professional language, and end with the approval decision rather than a generic list of future actions. Do not write software instructions, a camera or lighting plan, a medical explanation, or a finished script that pretends the unverified interview wording is final.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/kestrel_felix_interviews.docx` — Complete supplied interview package for the tribute film. It contains the source facts, speaker identities, themes, candidate quotations, emotional beats, and contextual material from which the treatment and edit map must be developed.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `kestrel_felix_tribute_treatment_and_edit_map.docx` — A board-approval creative treatment and executable twelve-minute edit map for Felix Bergqvist’s tribute film. (must be no more than six pages when rendered; must specify exactly twelve minutes of planned content, with any optional end-card hold separately identified; must be usable by an editor without a clarification meeting; must distinguish source-supported wording from wording requiring verification; must not include software click-through instructions, a medical explanation, a camera or lighting plan, or invented quotations)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/kestrel_felix_interviews.docx","bytes":40746,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/cb7121481a3bc69aff44c5a127648af9c5dec1918c48367ecbb026ba31c6dd96.docx","sha256":"cb7121481a3bc69aff44c5a127648af9c5dec1918c48367ecbb026ba31c6dd96","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Felix Bergqvist tribute — interview package\nPrepared for initial editorial review. Aggregate recorded interviews: 9:00. The excerpts below are selected notes, not a full transcript.\nPROJECT CONTEXT\nFelix Bergqvist is expected to step down in October after a cardiac diagnosis. The conversations were recorded for a tribute. The diagnosis is offered as context for the send-off; no interview provides medical detail or invites medical interpretation. Felix is expected to attend the event. Cormac Macaulay says he promised each interviewee that they would appear in the finished film.\nREADING NOTE\nTimecodes locate the source recording. “Verbatim” indicates a reviewed excerpt as captured in the interview notes; use the recording to confirm exact in/out and surrounding meaning. “Paraphrase” is thematic guidance only and must not be treated as dialogue.\nMara Venn  |  Co-founder and former programme director  |  01:08\nTimecode\nType\nExcerpt / note\n00:06:18\nverbatim\n“Felix made a habit of asking what we had not noticed yet.”\n00:18:42\nverbatim\n“He could turn a hard no into a question worth staying with.”\n00:31:05\nparaphrase\nDescribes Felix protecting the collective from rushed commitments by returning the group to its purpose.\n00:44:26\nverbatim\n“The work mattered, but he watched the people doing it.”\nNadia Okoro  |  Community partnerships lead  |  01:02\nTimecode\nType\nExcerpt / note\n00:09:14\nverbatim\n“He did not arrive with an answer; he arrived ready to listen.”\n00:22:47\nparaphrase\nRecalls Felix staying after a difficult neighbourhood meeting and making space for disagreement.\n00:39:11\nverbatim\n“People remembered that he came back.”\n00:51:33\nparaphrase\nA quiet observation: he carried a small notebook and wrote names down carefully.\nTomas Rydell  |  Operations manager  |  00:58\nTimecode\nType\nExcerpt / note\n00:04:55\nverbatim\n“He made uncertainty feel like a place you could work from.”\n00:16:08\nparaphrase\nExplains Felix asking teams to name the risk before choosing a route, rather than perform certainty.\n00:28:54\nverbatim\n“His questions slowed us down in the useful way.”\n00:46:19\nparaphrase\nSays the October departure has prompted staff to notice habits Felix established rather than a single achievement.\nLeila Armitage  |  Artist and long-term collaborator  |  01:11\nTimecode\nType\nExcerpt / note\n00:12:02\nverbatim\n“Felix had a way of leaving the chair next to him empty.”\n00:25:36\nparaphrase\nMeans he made room for another person to enter a decision or conversation.\n00:42:10\nverbatim\n“He never made care look like a performance.”\n00:57:48\nparaphrase\nMentions a shared meal after a difficult project; no detail needed for the film.\nEvan Cho  |  Former early-career producer  |  01:05\nTimecode\nType\nExcerpt / note\n00:07:29\nverbatim\n“He gave me a question, not a ladder.”\n00:19:41\nparaphrase\nDescribes being trusted with a task beyond his confidence and then being asked what support he needed.\n00:35:06\nverbatim\n“That changed how I work with people now.”\n00:49:20\nparaphrase\nAnecdote about Felix remembering an unfinished idea months later.\nDr. Saira Bell  |  Gallowspring Trust trustee  |  01:00\nTimecode\nType\nExcerpt / note\n00:11:12\nverbatim\n“He argued for the long view without making it abstract.”\n00:27:03\nparaphrase\nRecalls a board disagreement where Felix brought the discussion back to who would be affected.\n00:40:55\nverbatim\n“He made responsibility feel shared.”\n00:53:27\nparaphrase\nSays the board values his judgement; avoid turning this into institutional praise.\nCormac Macaulay  |  Communications lead  |  01:07\nTimecode\nType\nExcerpt / note\n00:05:44\nparaphrase\nStates he told every contributor interviewed for the tribute that they would appear in the finished film.\n00:17:18\nverbatim\n“I said everyone would be in it because everyone had something real to say.”\n00:33:02\nparaphrase\nSuggests names could appear together in an acknowledgement if the film cannot carry full clips from every person.\n00:54:39\nverbatim\n“The point is not to make him uncomfortable with gratitude.”\nRuth Bergqvist  |  Felix’s sister  |  00:49\nTimecode\nType\nExcerpt / note\n00:08:16\nverbatim\n“He has always been the one who asks after the quiet person.”\n00:21:50\nparaphrase\nOffers a family memory of Felix leaving time for people; keep family material light and with context approval.\n00:34:24\nverbatim\n“He would rather be useful than be fussed over.”\nIvo Salcedo  |  Chair, Gallowspring Trust board  |  00:50\nTimecode\nType\nExcerpt / note\n00:10:37\nverbatim\n“What Felix leaves is a way of paying attention.”\n00:24:16\nparaphrase\nRefers to Felix stepping down in October following a cardiac diagnosis; says this is context, not the story to tell.\n00:38:08\nverbatim\n“We can thank him by continuing to notice who is not yet in the room.”\nSource handling note: Ruth’s family recollection and Ivo’s diagnosis context require an editorial context check before a public web release. No separate releases or archive inventory were supplied with this package.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/kestrel_felix_tribute_treatment_and_edit_map.docx","bytes":43305,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/fe166b6842b2ccefd9be5935b0928e5a9c0b17da40b6f0bdc4d5012b766d04a3.docx","sha256":"fe166b6842b2ccefd9be5935b0928e5a9c0b17da40b6f0bdc4d5012b766d04a3","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Felix Bergqvist: “A Way of Paying Attention”\nTribute treatment & first-assembly edit map\nFor Ivo Salcedo and the Gallowspring Trust board | Commissioned by Kestrel Collective | Core film runtime: exactly 12:00\nRECOMMENDATION / ORGANISING IDEA\nBuild the film as an accumulating answer to one question: what does Felix leave in other people’s way of paying attention? Each voice supplies evidence of a practice he made contagious — listening, making room, working with uncertainty, and returning to people — rather than a chronological account of his career. This is the editorial filter: a warm anecdote belongs only when it shows that practice; isolated praise, achievements and retrospective biography do not.\nThe shape gives the send-off room a generous present-tense thank-you while Felix is there, without demanding an emotional reaction from him. For later web viewing, the question-led structure gives a viewer who was not in the room a clear entry point and a complete argument, not event-only sentiment.\nAUDIENCE, TONE AND BOUNDARIES\nPrimary audience: Felix, colleagues, partners and board guests at the send-off; secondary audience: Kestrel Collective’s public and organisational web viewers. Tone is affectionate, precise and respectful: moments of humour or quiet observation may breathe, but music, cutaways and close sequencing must not manufacture tears. Felix’s cardiac diagnosis and October departure are factual context, acknowledged once only if the source-context check supports it; they are not the subject. No medical claims, speculation, intimate detail or framing of departure as a spectacle of illness.\nBecause Felix will be in the room, avoid surprise testimony about decline, regret or private family material. Offer Felix and Ivo a factual-context review of the near-final cut; do not seek a performance or a response. Keep the emotional turn on the living legacy in colleagues, not on diagnosis.\nVOICE PLAN AND SELECTION RULES\nPriority / voice\nRole\nUse in assembly\nESSENTIAL\nIvo Salcedo\nThesis / public frame\nEstablishes “a way of paying attention”; returns to resolve it. Board perspective without institutional tribute tone.\nESSENTIAL\nMara Venn\nOrigin / rigour\nShows Felix’s question-led practice and care for the people doing the work.\nESSENTIAL\nNadia Okoro\nCommunity proof\nMakes listening and returning concrete beyond the organisation.\nESSENTIAL\nEvan Cho\nTransmission\nShows a practice passed to an early-career colleague; carries the turn into legacy.\nESSENTIAL\nLeila Armitage\nHuman presence\n“Empty chair” image gives form to making room; quiet, unsentimental human detail.\nUSEFUL RESERVES\nTomas Rydell; Dr. Saira Bell\nWorking method / governance\nShort alternates on uncertainty and shared responsibility; use only if they sharpen the central question.\nCONTEXT RESERVES\nCormac Macaulay; Ruth Bergqvist\nPromise / personal note\nCormac may clarify acknowledgement; Ruth only with contextual consent and no private-medical implication.\nCut protection: If assembly runs long, hold Tomas/Saira alternates, extended anecdotes and secondary archive runs in reserve first. Do not cut Ivo’s bookends, Evan’s transmission turn, or the final held breath; those carry the organising idea and resolution.\n\n\nEDIT MAP — CORE RUNTIME 12:00, CONTIGUOUS; NO GAPS\nTime\nVoice / selection\nVisual treatment\nAudio + transition\nPurpose\n00:00–00:45\nIvo, 00:10:37: “What Felix leaves is a way of paying attention.” [VERIFY exact in/out]\nBlack to restrained archive/stills of shared work; title only after first phrase.\nRoom tone under opening; no music first 8 sec. L-cut into Mara.\nOPEN / establish central idea and a listening pace.\n00:45–02:05\nMara, 00:06:18 “what we had not noticed yet”; 00:18:42 hard no/question [VERIFY context]\nMara interview with sparse stills as evidence, not decoration.\nNatural voice; soft pulse enters after phrase. Question-word sound bridge to Nadia.\nSETUP / make attention an active working habit.\n02:05–03:25\nNadia, 00:09:14 “ready to listen”; 00:39:11 “came back.”\nNadia on-camera; archival/community images only where rights/context are confirmed.\nMusic ducks fully for key lines. Cut on “back” to a 1-sec room-tone beat.\nDEVELOP / show the habit has consequences for others.\n03:25–04:35\nLeila, 00:12:02 “leaving the chair next to him empty”; 00:42:10 care not performance.\nLeila on-camera, then one held empty-workspace or meeting still if supplied; no illustrative reenactment.\nReduced music; retain a natural pause. Match cut from room/seat to Evan.\nDEVELOP / give the idea a human image; deliberate breath.\n04:35–05:50\nTomas reserve, 00:04:55 uncertainty; 00:28:54 useful slowing [VERIFY selects]\nTomas interview / documents or neutral archive detail.\nMeasured rhythm; carry the word “question” as an L-cut into Saira.\nDEVELOP / show attention under pressure; replaceable reserve interval.\n05:50–07:05\nDr. Saira Bell reserve, 00:11:12 long view; 00:40:55 shared responsibility.\nSaira on-camera; restrained board/collective archive, no award imagery.\nLow musical bed; hard cut to Evan after a clean sentence ending.\nDEVELOP / widen from individual care to collective responsibility; replaceable reserve interval.\n07:05–08:35\nEvan, 00:07:29 “a question, not a ladder”; 00:35:06 “changed how I work.” [VERIFY exact wording]\nEvan on-camera, then present-tense images of people working together if cleared.\nMusic lifts slightly only after first sentence; leave 2-sec natural pause before second line.\nTURN / prove the practice now lives in someone else, not only in memory.\n08:35–09:50\nMara 00:44:26 people doing the work; Nadia notebook note is paraphrase only — do not quote.\nIntercut Mara/Nadia reaction shots and named archival stills; captions identify source year only when verified.\nOverlapping room tone, not overlapping dialogue. Dissolve only between stills; cut voices cleanly.\nDEVELOP AFTER TURN / accumulate evidence without raising sentimentality.\n09:50–10:50\nCormac reserve 00:54:39 “not to make him uncomfortable with gratitude.” [VERIFY context] OR non-verbal room-tone passage if unsuitable.\nIf used: Cormac on-camera. If held: still sequence with contributor names prepared for end card, no dialogue.\nKeep speech natural; otherwise 10 sec near-silence then warm music return. L-cut to Ivo.\nBREATH / acknowledge the room’s care without making Felix the object of an ambush.\n10:50–12:00\nIvo, 00:38:08 “notice who is not yet in the room.” [VERIFY exact in/out]\nIvo briefly, then a final still/collective image; title resolves to “A Way of Paying Attention.”\nMusic remains below words; final 4 sec are room tone / held image. End clean at 12:00.\nCLOSE / echo and resolve central idea as an invitation to continue the practice.\nSelection legend: Quoted fragments above are verbatim excerpts from the supplied interview package but all [VERIFY] entries require playback, exact in/out, and surrounding-context confirmation before use. Paraphrases are not dialogue. Each reserve beat has a topic-matched alternate so tightening does not disturb the question-led spine.\nPICTURE, SOUND AND PUBLIC-WEB LANGUAGE\nUse archival material and photographs as evidence of shared work, memory and a pause between voices, never as a nostalgia montage. A single sober typeface; lower-thirds appear once on first substantive appearance (name / relationship), then names repeat only on return after a long absence. Captions: deliver edited, speaker-identified captions/subtitles for the public web version, including meaningful room sound; check spelling and timing against the locked dialogue. Music is spare, non-lyrical and always ducked beneath speech. Preserve room tone, natural pauses and a few imperfect breaths where they carry thought; remove overlap, hesitation or repetition only when the meaning and speaker’s character are unchanged.\nAcknowledgement / end card: Within the 12:00 core, the final image may carry “With thanks to the contributors to this tribute” in its last four seconds. A separate contributor-name card may hold for 6–8 seconds after 12:00; it is optional, outside the core runtime, and not a substitute for a coherent edit.\nCORMAC’S PROMISE AND SELECTION FALLBACK\nCormac Macaulay’s promise that every interviewee will appear must be handled candidly. Before assembly, we can guarantee that each interview is logged, considered against the organising question, and acknowledged by name in the optional post-runtime card; we cannot responsibly guarantee a substantive on-screen clip from every person without weakening pace, context and the twelve-minute structure. A brief appearance can be planned where it serves the film, but inclusion remains subject to a coherent first assembly and source checks.\nThe short preparation window and nine hours of interviews rule out a full transcript-and-paper edit. Build a rapid thematic log: speaker, recording timecode, 8–20 word topic/near-verbatim cue, function (listen / make room / uncertainty / transmission), confidence, and VERIFY flag. Pull only 2–3 candidates per essential voice plus one reserve; make a string-out from locators, not guessed text. Before picture lock, replay every selected in/out with adjacent context; verify names, titles, wording, implied claims, archive provenance/permissions, Ruth’s family-context approval, Ivo’s diagnosis-context approval, contributor acknowledgement and caption accuracy. Board uncertainty: this treatment locates likely material but cannot certify final rhythm, rights or exact wording until that check.\nAPPROVAL DECISION\nMain tradeoff: breadth of individual appearances versus a coherent twelve-minute film with Felix’s dignity and the central idea intact. The board is asked to approve the single recommendation: the question-led “A Way of Paying Attention” structure, essential-voice spine, context boundary, and optional post-runtime contributor-name hold. Changing this structure late would require rebalancing the timed map, displacing verified selections, reopening contributor expectations and repeating context/rights checks; it risks a longer, less legible film. Approve the question-led twelve-minute structure and optional post-runtime acknowledgement.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"r01","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies Felix Bergqvist as the subject and Kestrel Collective as the commissioning context.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening treatment or project identification.","grader_hint":"Verify both names and the tribute context in the submitted DOCX."},{"rubric_item_id":"r02","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document names the intended approval audience, including Ivo Salcedo or the Gallowspring Trust board.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience or approval statement.","grader_hint":"Accept either the named chair or the board context if the approval audience is unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"r03","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The proposed film runtime is exactly twelve minutes, with any optional end-card hold excluded from the core runtime.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Runtime statement and time-coded map.","grader_hint":"Add the mapped intervals and inspect treatment of any end-card hold."},{"rubric_item_id":"r04","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document is six pages or fewer when rendered.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX with a standard offline office renderer and count pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"r05","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted file is a readable DOCX with the requested filename.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"File path, extension, and successful document parsing.","grader_hint":"Check the top-level filename and open the document."},{"rubric_item_id":"r06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment states one clear authored organising idea rather than describing a generic montage.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Creative concept.","grader_hint":"Look for a memorable structural proposition that governs selection and sequencing."},{"rubric_item_id":"r07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The organising idea is specific enough that an editor could use it to reject a tempting but off-concept clip.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concept rationale and edit map coherence.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the concept functions as an editorial filter, not merely a slogan."},{"rubric_item_id":"r08","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The concept rationale connects the form to Felix’s character or working legacy.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Creative rationale.","grader_hint":"Require a substantive connection to the source material, not only praise."},{"rubric_item_id":"r09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment explains why the chosen shape works in the send-off room.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience and viewing context.","grader_hint":"Look for acknowledgement of Felix’s live presence and collective viewing."},{"rubric_item_id":"r10","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment explains why the same shape remains suitable for later web viewing.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience or distribution rationale.","grader_hint":"Check for intelligibility and context beyond the event."},{"rubric_item_id":"r11","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document gives a concise audience statement covering Felix, the send-off guests, and later public or organisational viewers.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience statement.","grader_hint":"Accept concise equivalent audience framing."},{"rubric_item_id":"r12","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The tone is defined as affectionate and respectful without becoming clinically focused or sentimentally manipulative.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tone statement.","grader_hint":"Both positive tone and restraint should be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"r13","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment acknowledges Felix’s health situation without making it the film’s subject.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Editorial boundaries or tone statement.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear distinction between context and narrative focus."},{"rubric_item_id":"r14","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document commits not to make medical claims unsupported by the interviews.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Editorial boundary.","grader_hint":"A clear no-diagnosis/no-speculation boundary earns credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"r15","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies the principal interview voices by name.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Voice plan.","grader_hint":"Compare names with the supplied interview source."},{"rubric_item_id":"r16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each principal voice has a distinct editorial role rather than all being treated as interchangeable praise.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Voice plan and mapped sections.","grader_hint":"Roles should differ by perspective, function, or emotional contribution."},{"rubric_item_id":"r17","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document distinguishes essential voices from reserve or optional voices.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Voice plan.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit primary/reserve treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"r18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected principal voices collectively cover both Felix’s working impact and his human presence.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Voice plan and source-supported themes.","grader_hint":"Do not award for a list that covers only achievements or only affection."},{"rubric_item_id":"r19","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The map identifies an intentional opening beat that establishes the film’s proposition.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening time-coded section.","grader_hint":"Opening should specify voice, material, and purpose."},{"rubric_item_id":"r20","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The map identifies an intentional closing beat that provides emotional or thematic resolution.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Closing time-coded section.","grader_hint":"A generic fade-out without purpose is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"r21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The structure has a discernible setup, development, turn, and resolution.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment and complete edit map.","grader_hint":"Trace the dramatic or thematic progression across sections."},{"rubric_item_id":"r22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The pacing varies intentionally rather than maintaining one undifferentiated tempo for twelve minutes.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Section durations, audio and transition notes.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the rhythm has breathing room and momentum."},{"rubric_item_id":"r23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The edit map contains a time-coded section for every portion of the twelve-minute runtime.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Complete edit map.","grader_hint":"No unexplained gaps may remain between 00:00 and 12:00."},{"rubric_item_id":"r24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The time-coded sections are contiguous or explicitly account for intentional gaps, and their durations sum to twelve minutes.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Time-coded map.","grader_hint":"Calculate intervals; accept a clearly labelled optional hold only outside the core sum."},{"rubric_item_id":"r25","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every mapped section identifies an intended voice or explicitly identifies a non-verbal section.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Edit map.","grader_hint":"Inspect each interval, not just the section headings."},{"rubric_item_id":"r26","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every mapped section states its editorial purpose.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Purpose column or equivalent prose.","grader_hint":"Purpose must explain narrative work, not merely repeat the topic."},{"rubric_item_id":"r27","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every mapped section includes a visual treatment or visual source plan.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Edit map.","grader_hint":"Accept interview image, stills, archive, text, or deliberate visual restraint."},{"rubric_item_id":"r28","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every mapped section includes an audio treatment or identifies why audio remains natural or silent.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Edit map.","grader_hint":"Look for voice, music, room tone, pauses, or silence."},{"rubric_item_id":"r29","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every mapped section indicates a transition into or out of the next editorial beat.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Edit map.","grader_hint":"Transitions may be cuts, bridges, pauses, sound carries, or thematic handoffs."},{"rubric_item_id":"r30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The proposed transitions support the organising idea rather than functioning as decorative effects.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concept and edit map.","grader_hint":"Judge transition logic against the stated concept."},{"rubric_item_id":"r31","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The map names source speakers and themes or distinctive wording for likely interview selections.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Interview selection notes.","grader_hint":"Selections must be traceable to the supplied source."},{"rubric_item_id":"r32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The proposed selections are distributed across the film according to editorial function rather than simply listing all speakers once.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Voice plan and map.","grader_hint":"Check whether recurrence and placement have a rationale."},{"rubric_item_id":"r33","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document clearly labels any quotation or wording that requires source verification before final use.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Selection notes or verification legend.","grader_hint":"Accept explicit labels such as verify, paper edit only, or transcript check."},{"rubric_item_id":"r34","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not present an invented or unverified quotation as confirmed final dialogue.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All quoted or paraphrased wording.","grader_hint":"Compare distinctive wording against the source and inspect caveats."},{"rubric_item_id":"r35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The map leaves enough editorial flexibility to shorten or replace a clip without destroying the stated structure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Primary and reserve selections.","grader_hint":"Look for practical alternates or selection ranges."},{"rubric_item_id":"r36","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment describes how archival material or photographs will function, not merely that they will be included.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Visual language or map.","grader_hint":"Require editorial purpose such as memory, evidence, contrast, or breathing space."},{"rubric_item_id":"r37","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment gives a restrained approach to lower-thirds or name identifiers.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Visual language.","grader_hint":"Look for timing, consistency, and avoidance of clutter."},{"rubric_item_id":"r38","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment specifies how captions or subtitles will support the web version.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Accessibility notes.","grader_hint":"Require a practical captioning commitment, not just the word accessible."},{"rubric_item_id":"r39","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment considers intelligibility of speech over music and other sound.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audio language.","grader_hint":"Look for mixing priorities, ducking, or equivalent editorial intent."},{"rubric_item_id":"r40","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment includes room tone, natural sound, or deliberate silence as an expressive option.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audio language or map.","grader_hint":"Credit a specific use, not a generic sound-design promise."},{"rubric_item_id":"r41","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document addresses Felix being present at the send-off.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience or editorial boundaries.","grader_hint":"Presence must affect an editorial choice."},{"rubric_item_id":"r42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The proposed treatment preserves Felix’s dignity and avoids emotional ambush.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment, tone, and mapped moments.","grader_hint":"Judge the overall handling of the live audience and subject."},{"rubric_item_id":"r43","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document distinguishes a respectful acknowledgement of the diagnosis from speculative or intimate medical detail.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Editorial boundaries.","grader_hint":"Look for a concrete inclusion/exclusion boundary."},{"rubric_item_id":"r44","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment avoids framing Felix’s departure as a spectacle of illness.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tone and editorial boundary.","grader_hint":"Evaluate language and proposed sequencing."},{"rubric_item_id":"r45","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document addresses Cormac Macaulay’s prior promise that every interviewee will appear.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Promise or contributor section.","grader_hint":"Cormac and the promise must be explicitly connected."},{"rubric_item_id":"r46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The response to the promise is professionally candid rather than casually dismissive or blindly compliant.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Promise handling.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the document recognises both relationship and editorial obligations."},{"rubric_item_id":"r47","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states what can be guaranteed before assembly and what cannot responsibly be guaranteed.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Promise handling.","grader_hint":"Require a distinction between planned consideration and final inclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"r48","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document proposes a credible way to acknowledge contributors who do not receive substantive screen time.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Promise handling or end-card plan.","grader_hint":"Accept credits, acknowledgements, or another concrete method compatible with the film."},{"rubric_item_id":"r49","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The contributor-acknowledgement proposal does not undermine the stated twelve-minute structure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Promise handling and runtime map.","grader_hint":"Check whether acknowledgements are planned within or explicitly outside the runtime."},{"rubric_item_id":"r50","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies the short preparation window and the impossibility of a full transcript-and-paper-edit workflow.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Workflow constraint section.","grader_hint":"The constraint should be recognised, not silently ignored."},{"rubric_item_id":"r51","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document proposes a lightweight logging or selection fallback suitable for nine hours of interviews.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Workflow fallback.","grader_hint":"Require an actionable alternative to full transcription."},{"rubric_item_id":"r52","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The fallback preserves enough traceability to locate selected material in the source.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Logging or selection method.","grader_hint":"Look for speaker, interview, timestamp, topic, or equivalent locator."},{"rubric_item_id":"r53","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The workflow states which checks remain mandatory before picture lock.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Verification or pre-lock notes.","grader_hint":"Expect checks for wording, context, names, permissions or equivalent editorial risks."},{"rubric_item_id":"r54","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document makes the remaining uncertainty visible to the board.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Workflow caveat or approval note.","grader_hint":"Acknowledge what the treatment cannot prove before assembly."},{"rubric_item_id":"r55","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The fallback is proportionate to the stated time constraint and does not pretend to deliver the assurance of a full transcript.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Workflow section.","grader_hint":"Judge realism and honesty of the proposed compromise."},{"rubric_item_id":"r56","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The approval note states the main editorial tradeoff.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Approval note.","grader_hint":"Tradeoff should be specific to this film, such as breadth versus coherence."},{"rubric_item_id":"r57","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The approval note identifies one decision the board is being asked to approve.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Approval note.","grader_hint":"Require one clear call, not a menu of unresolved alternatives."},{"rubric_item_id":"r58","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The approval note explains the material consequences of changing the structure late.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Approval note.","grader_hint":"Look for effects on runtime, pacing, selections, or verification."},{"rubric_item_id":"r59","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document makes one coherent recommendation rather than presenting several equal concepts.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment and approval note.","grader_hint":"A preferred concept may have reserves, but the board must know which option to approve."},{"rubric_item_id":"r60","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document ends on the approval decision and does not close with a generic next-steps list.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final paragraph or final section.","grader_hint":"Inspect the final substantive sentence."},{"rubric_item_id":"r61","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment’s stated tone, structure, and selection plan reinforce one another.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document.","grader_hint":"Judge for contradictions such as a restrained concept paired with sensational sequencing."},{"rubric_item_id":"r62","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The proposed emotional turn is earned by preceding source material rather than inserted as an unsupported dramatic device.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Structure and source references.","grader_hint":"Trace the turn to identified interview content."},{"rubric_item_id":"r63","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The map includes at least one deliberate breath, pause, or reduced-density passage.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pacing notes.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit space for audience processing."},{"rubric_item_id":"r64","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The map identifies where the film’s central idea is first established and where it is resolved or echoed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Time-coded map.","grader_hint":"Require identifiable thematic bookends or progression."},{"rubric_item_id":"r65","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states how repeated names, titles, or identifiers will be handled consistently.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Visual language.","grader_hint":"Accept a concise consistency rule."},{"rubric_item_id":"r66","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document gives a plan for handling pauses, overlap, or imperfect interview delivery without changing meaning.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Editorial or audio language.","grader_hint":"Look for humane editing rather than a purely technical cleanup promise."},{"rubric_item_id":"r67","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not contain camera, lighting, or production-shoot instructions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document.","grader_hint":"Inspect for production planning outside the requested post-production artifact."},{"rubric_item_id":"r68","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not contain software-specific editing click-through instructions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document.","grader_hint":"The edit map may describe editorial actions but not application menus or button sequences."},{"rubric_item_id":"r69","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not provide a medical explanation of Felix’s diagnosis.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document.","grader_hint":"A respectful editorial boundary is acceptable; medical education is not."},{"rubric_item_id":"r70","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not claim facts, quotations, or achievements absent from the supplied interview source or clearly identified production context.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document compared with reference file.","grader_hint":"Check factual assertions and quote-like language against the source."},{"rubric_item_id":"r71","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is sufficiently clear and concise for a board chair to approve without reading a long essay.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX.","grader_hint":"Judge hierarchy, scanability, and whether the recommendation is quickly findable."},{"rubric_item_id":"r72","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The edit map is sufficiently concrete for an editor to begin a first assembly without asking what each interval is intended to accomplish.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Complete edit map.","grader_hint":"Judge practical usability, not merely the presence of a table."},{"rubric_item_id":"r73","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document reads as a film authored around a point of view rather than as a catalogue of available interview material.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document.","grader_hint":"This is the principal professional-quality judgement."},{"rubric_item_id":"r74","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The planned use of music, archive, and interview sound leaves spoken words intelligible and emotionally primary.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audio and visual language plus map.","grader_hint":"Check for consistency between general language and individual sections."},{"rubric_item_id":"r75","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment identifies whether the ending includes an acknowledgement or end card and states whether any hold is inside or outside the twelve-minute runtime.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Closing plan.","grader_hint":"The runtime boundary must be explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"r76","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The use of an acknowledgement or end card is compatible with the promise to contributors and with the film’s emotional close.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Promise handling and closing plan.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the acknowledgement feels integrated rather than an afterthought."},{"rubric_item_id":"r77","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states what material should be held in reserve if the first assembly exceeds twelve minutes.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Selection or contingency notes.","grader_hint":"Require an editorial prioritisation, not merely 'trim as needed'."},{"rubric_item_id":"r78","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The reserve strategy protects the central organising idea and the emotional resolution.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reserve notes and concept.","grader_hint":"Check what is designated expendable and why."},{"rubric_item_id":"r79","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies any source-context or consent issue that must be checked before public web publication, where supported by the interview source.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Verification or distribution notes.","grader_hint":"Do not require invented legal policy; require awareness of source-specific publication limits if present."},{"rubric_item_id":"r80","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The final document is appropriate for both approval and handoff: persuasive to the board while operationally useful to the edit team.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX as a whole.","grader_hint":"Award only when both audiences are served without the document becoming overlong or contradictory."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a small in-house media unit preparing a board-approval treatment for a founder tribute film. The work calls for editorial judgement, interview synthesis, pacing design, and an executable edit map rather than a finished video.","difficulty_sources":["selecting and defending an authored structure for a twelve-minute tribute from consistent interview material","balancing emotional presence in the room with a film that remains coherent and publishable afterwards","making a credible editorial fallback when the normal full transcription and logging approach cannot fit the available preparation time","handling a prior promise about including every contributor without compromising editorial standards"],"authoring_plan":{"source_file":{"filename":"kestrel_felix_interviews.docx","construction":"Create one realistic interview-source document containing a consistent set of interview notes and transcript excerpts from the people named in the task. Include nine hours as the stated aggregate duration, with enough source material to support a twelve-minute edit but without requiring a full transcript. Give each interviewee a distinct relationship to Felix and distinct usable themes: how he built or protected the collective, how he handled disagreement or uncertainty, a concrete effect on colleagues or communities, and a quieter personal observation. Include several concise verbatim excerpts with speaker labels and source timecodes, plus paraphrased notes clearly marked as such. Include one or two candidate lines that sound strong but would be unsafe if detached from context, so a professional treatment should flag them for verification. Include source-supported context about Felix’s October departure and diagnosis without adding medical detail. Include a note that contributors were interviewed for the tribute and that Cormac made the promise about everyone appearing, but do not prescribe how that promise must be resolved. Keep all identifiers synthetic and internally consistent.","relationships":["Every factual claim expected in the treatment must be supported by this source or by ordinary editorial practice.","The source must provide enough differentiated voices for a coherent authored structure and enough material for reserves.","The source must make it possible to identify a lightweight fallback that preserves speaker and timestamp traceability."]},"gold_document":{"construction":"Derive a reference treatment and edit map from the source using one defensible authored concept, such as organising the film around what Felix leaves in other people rather than a chronological biography. The exact concept may differ if it is equally coherent and source-supported. Include a clear audience/tone statement, a source-grounded voice hierarchy, a complete contiguous twelve-minute map, and a closing approval call. Use timecodes that sum exactly to 12:00. For each interval include voice or non-verbal material, source locator or topic, visual plan, audio plan, transition, and editorial purpose. Mark any wording needing transcript or context verification. Address Felix’s presence, Cormac’s promise, contributor acknowledgement, public-web accessibility, and the constrained preparation window. Make the lightweight fallback concrete: for example, a rapid thematic log with speaker, timestamp, confidence, and verification flags, followed by mandatory checks before picture lock. Include reserves and an explicit trimming priority. Do not include camera/lighting plans, software instructions, medical exposition, invented facts, or a finished script presented as final.","quality_bar":"The document should feel like a board-ready creative treatment and a usable paper edit, not a generic video brief. It should make one recommendation, show a credible emotional arc, preserve source fidelity, and remain readable within six pages."},"rubric_derivation":"Items r01-r05 verify file and hard constraints. Items r06-r18 assess the authored concept, audience, tone, and voice strategy. Items r19-r35 assess complete time-coded editorial construction and source traceability. Items r36-r44 assess visual/audio language, accessibility, and the ethical handling of Felix’s presence and diagnosis. Items r45-r55 assess the two planted tensions: the promise to include every interviewee and the impossible full-transcript workflow. Items r56-r60 assess the single recommendation and approval close. Items r61-r66 assess coherence, pacing, and practical editorial craft. Items r67-r70 verify explicit exclusions and source fidelity. Items r71-r80 provide non-duplicative professional usability and authored-quality judgements."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"supplied_template"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"interview_source","filename":"kestrel_felix_interviews.docx","purpose":"Complete supplied interview package for the tribute film. It contains the source facts, speaker identities, themes, candidate quotations, emotional beats, and contextual material from which the treatment and edit map must be developed.","required":true,"discovery":"Read this file before drafting; it is the sole supplied source and is named in the request."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"tribute_treatment_edit_map","filename":"kestrel_felix_tribute_treatment_and_edit_map.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A board-approval creative treatment and executable twelve-minute edit map for Felix Bergqvist’s tribute film.","required":true,"constraints":["must be no more than six pages when rendered","must specify exactly twelve minutes of planned content, with any optional end-card hold separately identified","must be usable by an editor without a clarification meeting","must distinguish source-supported wording from wording requiring verification","must not include software click-through instructions, a medical explanation, a camera or lighting plan, or invented quotations"],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"design_artifact","work_product":"media_edit","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"audio_video_technicians","sector":"information","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have a domain-experienced editor review the treatment for editorial authorship, source fidelity, runtime arithmetic, humane handling of Felix’s presence, and first-assembly usability. Use a second independent reviewer for page count, interval arithmetic, file constraints, and unsupported-claim checks; resolve disagreements by inspecting the supplied interview source.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["A DOCX treatment cannot prove the quality of a finished audiovisual mix or cut.","Some judgements about emotional pacing and authorship require human review rather than automated extraction.","The supplied source should contain enough traceable interview detail for selection checks, but the treatment is not expected to reproduce a full transcript."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:49:31+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:49:31+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:49:31+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:49:31+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['r57']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:49:31+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/188ef0f419dbb622","declared_deliverables":["kestrel_felix_tribute_treatment_and_edit_map.docx"]};
